<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al Mahatta Fort</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Al Mahatta Museum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al Mahatta Fort is a fortified rest house built in Sharjah in 1932 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-airways">Imperial Airways</a> for passengers on its air route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The airfield beside it was the first in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a> and was used by civil and military aircraft for decades. The building was restored in 2000 as the Al Mahatta Museum, devoted to the history of aviation in the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mahatta-fort.jpg" alt="Al Mahatta Fort" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexandermcnabb · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Mahatta_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
